{"candidates":[{"content":"The claim in SRC_003 regarding the 'Checklist Paradox' integrates with the 'Averaging Error' hypothesis (SCR_004); specifically, the decay in verification quality caused by cognitive load in panels is offset by the law of large numbers, where random errors of non-experts cancel out, whereas the expert's failure is systematic. This relation is refuted if the panel's error distribution is found to be skewed (systematic) rather than Gaussian (random).","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"52794f9baed044e7e1f8200b49209a67023ee1551ed065c929e7cdd5845570b2"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"The 'cognitive ceiling' mechanism described in SRC_001 contradicts the 'Semantic Drift' hypothesis (SCR_007). While SRC_001 posits that checklists restrict detection to a predefined scope, Semantic Drift suggests that non-experts' divergent interpretations of those same prompts actually expand the detection scope beyond the expert's specialized blindness. This relation is refuted if non-experts' interpretations of checklist items converge perfectly with the expert's intended meaning.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"632483375614efe75c635eb9d519e21c21a05371c136505d0af98e79a8ed7391"}],"typicality":0.3},{"content":"The mechanism of 'compressed experience' in SRC_005 depends on the 'Expert-as-Process' framework (SCR_006), where the reliability of the panel is actually a distributed execution of a single expert's intuition codified into the checklist. This implies the panel does not possess independent reliability but inherits it via the checklist's authorship. This relation is refuted if the panel identifies an anomaly that was neither in the checklist nor detectable by the original expert author.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"ee142b5e8d6e43c7a8fe46d85f0f68c067692a904bb51165b5f615ed9699d9dc"}],"typicality":0.6},{"content":"The 'pattern recognition of silent failures' in SRC_004 abstracts to 